 ## Overview
-The Autograd Wrapper for Volume Rendering is a Python `torch.autograd.Function` implementation that replaces a previous C++-based autograd layer to route between forward and backward C++/CUDA operations while handling gradient requirements [S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of integrating differentiable volume rendering into deep learning pipelines by managing the dispatch logic for gradient computation, allowing gradients to flow from 2D projection losses back to 3D volume parameters [S2, unofficial]. It differs from lower-level C++ implementations by handling device placement and tensor management automatically, serving as the primary entry point for users [S2, unofficial].
+The Autograd Wrapper for Volume Rendering is a Python `torch.autograd.Function` implementation that serves as the interface between PyTorch's automatic differentiation engine and the underlying C++/CUDA volume rendering kernels. It solves the problem of integrating differentiable volume rendering into deep learning pipelines by routing execution to either a backward-aware CUDA kernel (when gradients are required) or an inference-optimized forward-only kernel (when they are not) [S2, unofficial]. It replaces the previous C++-based autograd layer, moving the gradient management logic into Python to reduce compilation overhead and improve maintainability [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The wrapper implements the `torch.autograd.Function` interface, specifically the `forward` and `backward` static methods, to manage the execution of volume rendering kernels and the propagation of gradients [S2, unofficial].
+The wrapper implements the `torch.autograd.Function` interface, defining `forward` and `backward` static methods. It interacts with the C++ library `fvdb` via Python bindings (`_fvdb_cpp`) exposed in `src/python/Bindings.cpp` [S2, unofficial].
 
